hello my name is frank bergans and the  title of my talk is characterization and  scale translation of a parallel  microbioreactor system for psychiatrist  development  this work is part of an engineering  doctorate project between ucl  biochemical engineering  and aplicon biotechnology the aim of the  project  is to establish the micromatrix a  screening tool and scale down model for  cell culture applications  in this talk i will present the work on  the engineering characterization of the  micromatrix focusing on  mixing time and kla  then i will demonstrate how matched  mixing time  in combination with a similar co2  edition profile can be used to scale  down a fat bread process  using a gstr cell line producing an  igg4 antibody from a stair tank  benchtop scale reactor to the shaken  micro matrix system  subsequently the high throughput of the  microbio reactor will be used to perform  a doe assisted optimization of the  feeding strategy  lastly the optimize fat batch protocol  will be  translated to the benchtop scale  in a typical process development  timeline clone screening and selection  is usually done  in these 96 well plates  promising clones are then transferred to  shake flask where the medium composition  can be optimized  the process is then transferred to a  benchtop sphere  stair tank reactor where process  parameters such as  ph and dissolved oxygen can be actively  controlled  finally the translation to pilot  and production scale follows  both report and the amount of  information that can be obtained from a  run  are functions of the scale the smaller  the scale the more experiments can be  run in power layer but also less  information is obtained  at the larger scale financial and space  constraints  restrict the number of parallel  experiments  however these experiments allow for  better insight  at conditions closer to the actual  production process  microbio reactors like the micro matrix  shown here  try to shake up this inverse  relationship by offering a fully  controlled culture environment at small  scale that allows for a high degree of  parallelization  the micromatrix is designed around  a 24 deep square well microtiter plate  as shown here on the right you can see a  close-up  of one of the 24 welds each of the welds  has a filtered gas  inlet for the addition of up to four  gases  a gas outlet and a liquid line which  allows for feed addition  but also ph control on the bottom  of the well are sensors for the  measurement of phdo  and temperature  the microtiter plate sits inside the  micromatrix on an  inbuilt orbital shaker  and engineering characterization of the  micromatrix was conducted in order to  provide the basis for the scale down of  an  existing fat batch process  in a 5 liter stair tank reactor to the  micro matrix and the shaking  24 standard round valve format  this photo shows a setup of the mixing  time experiments  using a mock-up well of the same  dimension  as the micro matrix cassette welds  the well was fixed onto an orbital  shaker  the color change of a dual indicator  system for mixing time  solution was used to determine the  mixing time inside the well  under dynamic conditions pictures were  taken using a high-speed camera  mounted on the shaking platform shown  here  the mixing time was determined by  analyzing the pictures and visually  observing the color change  mixing time tests were performed for  different values of  volume and steering speed  the mixing time as  function of the rotational speed as  shown in the figure here was found to  range  between 1 and 42 seconds an  increased working volume generally led  to longer mixing times whereas increased  shaking speeds shorten  mixing times this  is an expected result and it also agrees  with previous studies where a similar  trend was demonstrated  as shown in this table for different  miniature bioreactor systems  this photo shows the setup to determine  the volumetric oxide atmospheric  coefficient  kla measurements were performed using  the static guessing out method  to perform the measurement with a high  frequency  and with a sufficiently short probe  response time  a needle type oxygen microsensor  was used as shown in this photograph  the probe was inserted through the  off-gas filter of the assembled  micromatrix cassette  all dissolved oxygen was first removed  from the liquid through overlay with  nitrogen  the headspace was then flushed  before the operating conditions with  regard to airflow rate and shaking speed  were set and recording commenced  the results are illustrated in these  figures where the kla is plotted as  function of shaking speeds for three  different  air flow rates  the values of the kla ranged  between 2 and 240 depending on the  operating conditions  used the mass transfer is directly  proportional to the surface area of the  liquid  with increasing shaking speeds the  liquid surface  gradually deformed and its inclination  grew  which led to larger liquid surface area  and therefore higher kla values  the kla had an inverse relationship to  the liquid volume and in turn decreased  with increasing  working volume however there was little  effect  of changing the airflow weight on the  kla  as you can see from these graphs  there are four different criteria that  are commonly used for scale translation  of their culture processes  the first is based on matched tip speed  however this is not applicable for  scaling between  shaken and steered systems the second is  based on matched power per unit volume  but this value can only be derived via  simulation  or elaborate experimental setup for  shaken  systems this leaves matched  kla or matched mixing time  as potential candidates for scale  translation between stair tank reactor  and the micromatrix  this table shows the operating  conditions  for the three different cultivation  system  used in this study a kla value  of one per hour is often cited as being  sufficient  to support cell densities of up to one  times ten to the seven  cells per milliliter and all cultivation  platforms fulfill  this requirement therefore a matched  mixing time of approximately 6 seconds  was chosen as criterion for the scale  translation from shaken microwave  systems to the benchtop scale  this figure shows the viable cell  concentration and viability of a  g-stroke cell grown  in the 5-liter stair tank reactor and  the micromatrix system  also the viability is very similar the  maximum viable cell concentration is  more than double  in the micro matrix compared to the five  liter  reactor in contrast  the igg4 antibody tita  is almost identical the results suggest  that similar mixing conditions alone do  not promote  similar cell growth closer inspection  of the carbon dioxide edition profile  showed large  differences between the stair tank  reactor  and the micro matrix this figure  shows the co2 is continuously  added via overlay in the micromatrix  due to the manual bolder's additions of  base by contrast in the five liter stair  tank reactor  co2 addition stopped after three days  due to lactate formation further  acidification is counteracted by base  addition when lactate is consumed  towards the end of the run  co2 addition started again as shown in  the graph  such as co2 edition profile was emulated  in the micro matrix by using the  automated feeding module  instead of manual bolus additions of  base  as you can see for both co2 it  conditions the growth and product  profiles  are comparable between the 5  litre stair tank reactor and the micro  matrix  furthermore the maximum  viable cell concentration did not show  significant differences for these  systems  also the growth of cells cultivated in  the  24 standard round wealth was slightly  slower the trend was comparable to the  cultivation that used  gas plants with five percent co2  similarly the maximum viable cell  concentration in the  24 standard round wells was reached at  the later stage of the cultivation  but was not significantly different  to either the micro matrix or five liter  stair tank reactor  in addition the final title was not  significantly different  between the scales and co2 conditions  this indicates a difference in the cell  specific  productivity caused by the difference  of the co2 concentrations in the  inflowing gas  this table shows a summary of the  results from the previous slide  confirming the impact of the co2 edition  profile and growth and product  formation in the micro matrix and 5  liter  stair tank bioreactor systems it also  shows a high degree of similarity  between the two controlled bioreactor  systems  for all derived parameters by contrast  some  larger differences in growth rate and  final tita  were found when cells were grown in the  24  standard round wealth system  in order to ascertain whether the n-link  glycosylation of the igg4  antibody was scalable the distribution  of key glycoprofiles was compared  as you can see here the percentage of  forecast related glycans  was above 95 percent at all scales  which was similar to earlier studies of  the same  cell line and product  the distribution of cyalated  antibodies was similar for the control  systems  but reduced further 24  standard round well format as you can  see in this graph  similarly galactosylation was  comparable for all conditions run in the  micromatrix and five liter stair tank  reactor  but considerably reduced for antibodies  that were produced  in the 24 standard round welds  this was likely caused by a difference  in the  ph profile because  cultures in the 24 standard round welds  were not ph controlled  in summary the results show that a match  mixing time in conjunction with a  similar  co2 edition profile proved to be a  suitable scaling criterium to translate  a fat batch process from a conventional  stair tank  bioreactor to the micromatrix system  demonstrated the scale translation of a  fat batch process we used the  micromatrix to optimize the bolus  feeding strategy  a design of experiment approach with a  circumscribed central composite design  was employed to investigate three  factors  these were the total feet volume and  percentage of the initial working volume  the feet start in days after inoculation  and feed weight in number of bolus  additions at the feed volume  was divided all were investigated  at five different levels the center  point was replicated six  times this resulted in 20 runs  in total performed in a single  experiment  the feeding regime was optimized for the  space time yield instead of the final  titer  in order to avoid a bias towards  long process run times  the contour plot here shows  the optimal space-time yield was  captured within the investigated design  space  the optimal value of 71 milligrams per  day and liter  was predicted for a feed start after  five days in the total feed volume  of 64 percent that was divided into six  bolus additions  the predicted optimal bolus feeding  regime was repeated in the micromatrix  for validation  and then scaled up to the benchtop  bioreactor  the comparison to the original fat batch  protocol  shown as historic in grey was  made to assess the success of the  optimization  the results show minimal differences of  the growth kinetics between the scales  for the optimized feeding regime  in comparison to the original feeding  strategy  the moderate increase of the maximum  viable cell concentration and the  prolonged stationary  phase were observed  similarly the progression of the tita  was nearly identical between the scales  for the optimized feeding regime  and significantly higher compared  to the original fat batch regime as  shown  in this graph  these results are confirmed by the  comparison of the derived parameters  that were almost identical between the  micro matrix and the 5 liter stair tank  reactor under optimized  conditions whereas the values  for the original feeding regime showed a  substantially reduced  productivity as shown in this table  the final title and space-time yield of  the  optimized feeding protocol was about  20 percent higher compared to the  original protocol  and very close to the predicted optimum  in conclusion initially  an engineering characterization  of mixing time and kla was performed to  gain  an understanding of the cultivation  environment generated by the system  mixing and kla were found to be  comparable to other  microbioreactor systems and lab scale  bioreactors  based on the information obtained from  the engineering characterization  the mixing time was selected to remain  constant between  the 5 liter sdr micromatrix  and 24 stand around well to ensure  comparable  hydrodynamic conditions furthermore the  growth kinetics of the used cell lines  were highly dependent  on the co2 addition profile  as a result the concentration of co2 in  the inflow and gas  was also matched between the shaken  micro matrix and the five liters their  tank reactor  leading to very similar growth in  product kinetics  as well as glycosylation profiles for  both  co2 conditions  the high throughput of the micromatrix  was then leveraged to optimize the bolus  feeding strategy within the framework  of a circumscribed central composite  design  a scale up of the optimized feeding  regime demonstrated nearly identical  growth and production kinetics  between the micrometrics and the  benchtop scale  reactor and resulted in a 20 increase of  the space-time yield compared to the  original  feeding regime the results demonstrate  the potential of the micromatrix system  a scaled-down model  for fat batch cell culture processes and  its application for  process optimization  all the work presented was carried out  by vincent weakman  with support of dr gregorio  rodriguez and miss e lee in setting up  the mixing time experiments  i would also like to thank aplicon  biotechnology for  provision of the micromatrix system  and financial support of the nhd  studentship i would like to thank ludga  limited for their help with the  glycosylation analysis in lonza uk  for provision of the gs show cell line  this work was supported by the uk  engineering and physical science  research council as part of an  engineering doctorate training center  grant  in bioprocess engineering leadership  finally thank you for listening to my  presentation  and i'm happy to answer any of your  questions
 

